      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To demonstrate that the deep infiltration of the pudendal nerve guided by tomography is a good treatment option for patients with refractory neuralgia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Two cases of pudendal neuralgia are presented, both expressed mainly with pain in the perineal and gluteal areas. Both cases had changes in the skin and one with urinary symptoms. A deep trans-gluteal infiltration guided by CT scan was performed, administering bupivacaine 0.25% with 80 mg methylprednisolone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In women, after infiltration, there was a decrease in pain from 6 to 3. In man infiltrations were performed monthly, completing 4. He reported pain reduction from 8 to 2."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Pudendal Neuralgia diagnosis is unknown. The most common cause is inflammation of adjacent structures to the nerve frequently caused by falling. Diagnosis is mainly clinical. Trans-gluteal infiltration guided by CT scan is an effective option in treatment."
        }
      ]},
